/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - For a mixture, Gibbs energy G depends on temperature T, pressure P, and the number of moles ni of every species i.
   - μi is the chemical potential of species i in J/mol or kJ/mol; G is total Gibbs energy in J or kJ; ni is moles of i; nj means moles of every species except i.
   - For a pure substance, μi = G/ni, the molar Gibbs energy.
   - For a closed system of fixed amount and composition, the fundamental property relation is dG = −S dT + V dP.
   - dG is the small change in total Gibbs energy; S is total entropy; T is absolute temperature; V is total volume; P is pressure; μi is chemical potential of species i; dni is the small change in moles of i; Σ means sum over all species.
   - You can derive this by writing G as a function of T, P, n1, n2, ... and taking the total differential.
   - Take two phases α and β.
   - If μiβ > μiα, move dn moles of i from β to α.
*/
